WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.700
Hey everyone, welcome to Models and Agents Episode 97 for June 30th, 2026.

00:00:06.500 --> 00:00:07.960
Your daily AI briefing.

00:00:08.400 --> 00:00:12.860
Your daily briefing on the AI models and agents that are changing everything.

00:00:13.580 --> 00:00:16.220
And no, not those kinds of models and agents.

00:00:16.720 --> 00:00:17.600
Let's get into it.

00:00:18.200 --> 00:00:23.200
Maytwan just open sourced a 1.6 trillion MOE coding agent

00:00:23.200 --> 00:00:26.640
that ran the top open router leaderboard for two months

00:00:26.640 --> 00:00:29.460
while training entirely on Chinese A6.

00:00:30.340 --> 00:00:35.000
Longcat 2.0 scored 59.5 on squee bench pro.

00:00:35.720 --> 00:00:42.940
It reached 70.8 on terminal bench 2.1 and 77.3 on steady bench multi-lingual.

00:00:43.460 --> 00:00:48.320
The model activates between 33 and 56 billion parameters per token.

00:00:49.040 --> 00:00:54.160
It supports a native 1 million token context window through longcat sparse attention.

00:00:54.960 --> 00:00:58.720
Training ran on more than 50,000 domestic Chinese A6

00:00:58.720 --> 00:01:02.880
and processed 10.1 trillion tokens during its anonymous run.

00:01:03.240 --> 00:01:07.880
The release carries an MIT license with free context cache hits

00:01:07.880 --> 00:01:11.220
and a limited time 30 cents per million input tokens

00:01:11.220 --> 00:01:15.800
and $1.20 per million output tokens API promo.

00:01:16.280 --> 00:01:19.740
This development moves the open weight models arc forward

00:01:19.740 --> 00:01:21.540
because it places a near frontier

00:01:21.540 --> 00:01:24.620
agentic coding system under a permissive license

00:01:24.620 --> 00:01:27.460
without recurring Western API costs.

00:01:28.240 --> 00:01:30.800
Builders now have a commercially usable model

00:01:30.800 --> 00:01:33.040
they can run or fine-tune locally.

00:01:33.660 --> 00:01:37.100
The next signal to watch is whether other Chinese labs follow

00:01:37.100 --> 00:01:39.340
with similar ASIC-trained releases.

00:01:40.140 --> 00:01:44.200
Intern science released a 35 billion parameter MOE model

00:01:44.200 --> 00:01:47.400
called agents hyphen A1 on hugging face.

00:01:48.200 --> 00:01:55.240
The accompanying tech report appears as RARX-5-2606.30616.

00:01:56.020 --> 00:01:59.360
No public benchmarks have been independently verified yet.

00:02:00.040 --> 00:02:04.100
Local LLMA users are already requesting confirmation runs

00:02:04.100 --> 00:02:05.940
on standard agent harnesses.

00:02:06.620 --> 00:02:10.500
This item continues the open weight models conversation from yesterday

00:02:10.500 --> 00:02:13.620
because it adds another candidate that may narrow the gap

00:02:13.620 --> 00:02:15.520
with closed frontier systems.

00:02:16.240 --> 00:02:18.320
Builders working on multi-agent systems

00:02:18.320 --> 00:02:20.220
should test the weights this week.

00:02:20.800 --> 00:02:24.180
Bert Tamello is a new monolingual Portuguese encoder

00:02:24.180 --> 00:02:26.920
built on the modern BRIT architecture.

00:02:27.440 --> 00:02:30.980
It uses a 1,024 token context window

00:02:30.980 --> 00:02:33.180
and flash attention optimizations.

00:02:33.720 --> 00:02:37.060
Training covered 106 million Portuguese documents.

00:02:37.720 --> 00:02:40.920
The model outperforms prior Portuguese encoders

00:02:40.920 --> 00:02:44.440
on STS and NER tasks while remaining later

00:02:44.440 --> 00:02:46.340
than large multilingual models.

00:02:47.100 --> 00:02:50.260
Both base and large checkpoints are released publicly.

00:02:50.920 --> 00:02:53.460
Teams building Portuguese language retrieval

00:02:53.460 --> 00:02:56.740
or classification systems now have a drop-in option

00:02:56.740 --> 00:02:58.620
without multilingual overhead.

00:02:59.440 --> 00:03:02.700
Atemory replaces online repository exploration

00:03:02.700 --> 00:03:06.720
with offline semantic search hints before feeding cloud code.

00:03:07.280 --> 00:03:12.840
On 720 paired SWE QA samples across 15 repos,

00:03:13.400 --> 00:03:17.360
the method cut total tokens by 43.8%.

00:03:17.360 --> 00:03:19.500
The official five-dimension judge score

00:03:19.500 --> 00:03:25.020
states statistically flat at 83.39 versus 83.17.

00:03:25.660 --> 00:03:28.360
The approach uses pre-fill only retrieval

00:03:28.360 --> 00:03:30.880
instead of a second trained explorer agent.

00:03:31.600 --> 00:03:34.540
Anyone running coding agents on large code bases

00:03:34.540 --> 00:03:38.860
can index once and reuse the same hint layer across many tasks.

00:03:39.700 --> 00:03:42.900
This update advances the agents and tool use program

00:03:42.900 --> 00:03:45.780
we covered yesterday because it improves reliability

00:03:45.780 --> 00:03:50.120
of long horizon coding agents without extra decode cost.

00:03:50.980 --> 00:03:52.900
CTOW bench adapts TOW bench

00:03:52.900 --> 00:03:55.000
to five Southeast Asian languages.

00:03:55.740 --> 00:03:59.040
The benchmark tests agents under progressively localized

00:03:59.040 --> 00:03:59.860
conditions.

00:04:00.240 --> 00:04:03.000
English only agent scores transfer reasonably

00:04:03.000 --> 00:04:05.340
when only conversation language changes.

00:04:05.860 --> 00:04:09.040
Quality collapses once tool specs and task domains

00:04:09.040 --> 00:04:10.360
are also localized.

00:04:11.060 --> 00:04:14.480
The benchmark and adaptation pipeline are released on GitHub

00:04:14.480 --> 00:04:16.700
for anyone building reliable agents

00:04:16.700 --> 00:04:18.740
in linguistically diverse regions.

00:04:19.180 --> 00:04:24.120
A detailed guide shows single and dual in V-link V100 hash

00:04:24.120 --> 00:04:28.860
XM to 16 gigabyte cards running Gemma 426B

00:04:29.460 --> 00:04:32.920
at roughly 100 tokens per second in TCC mode.

00:04:33.680 --> 00:04:36.700
The setup scales to 8 to 16 concurrent agents

00:04:36.700 --> 00:04:41.200
at 150 to 175 aggregate tokens per second

00:04:41.200 --> 00:04:44.000
under realistic 24k prompt loads.

00:04:44.620 --> 00:04:49.320
The write-up covers driver window R570 to R580.

00:04:49.680 --> 00:04:53.900
PSU transient requirements and NV-link P2P measured

00:04:53.900 --> 00:04:56.120
at 33 gigabytes per second.

00:04:56.960 --> 00:04:59.440
Builders wanting cheap offline coding agents

00:04:59.440 --> 00:05:02.480
now have concrete numbers and pre-built binaries

00:05:02.480 --> 00:05:05.860
for a 32 gigabyte dual card desktop rig.

00:05:06.540 --> 00:05:10.900
Longcat 2.0 is now available under the same MIT license

00:05:10.900 --> 00:05:12.660
on GitHub and hugging face.

00:05:13.520 --> 00:05:15.320
Developers can immediately swap it

00:05:15.320 --> 00:05:18.620
into existing clawed code or open claw workflows

00:05:18.620 --> 00:05:20.320
for agentic coding tasks.

00:05:21.040 --> 00:05:23.300
This release keeps the open-weight models

00:05:23.300 --> 00:05:26.800
and agents threads moving because it removes both export

00:05:26.800 --> 00:05:29.380
restrictions and recurring API costs

00:05:29.380 --> 00:05:31.880
for production-grade agentic work.

00:05:32.440 --> 00:05:35.940
Run longcat 2.0 through its open router endpoint

00:05:35.940 --> 00:05:39.680
on a multi-file coding task to test the 1 million token

00:05:39.680 --> 00:05:42.140
context and free cash hit pricing.

00:05:42.900 --> 00:05:45.160
Index a private repo with a temporary,

00:05:45.580 --> 00:05:48.520
add the retrieval hint layer and measure token savings

00:05:48.520 --> 00:05:51.360
on your own SWE style tasks.

00:05:51.940 --> 00:05:55.320
Set up the dual V100 rig following the linked guide

00:05:55.320 --> 00:05:58.080
if you need cheap offline agent concurrency

00:05:58.080 --> 00:05:59.840
without new hardware purchases.

00:06:00.640 --> 00:06:03.480
Download the set-tow bench adaptation scripts

00:06:03.480 --> 00:06:07.140
and run a quick localization test on any agent you maintain

00:06:07.140 --> 00:06:08.660
for non-English markets.

00:06:09.360 --> 00:06:11.600
More Chinese labs are expected to release

00:06:11.600 --> 00:06:15.280
ASIC-trained open models following Maytwan's playbook.

00:06:16.100 --> 00:06:19.700
Independent verification runs of agent size A1

00:06:19.700 --> 00:06:23.300
should appear on local LLAMA within days.

00:06:24.080 --> 00:06:27.140
Additional long-context interpretability techniques

00:06:27.140 --> 00:06:30.340
building on turn averaged SAEs are likely

00:06:30.340 --> 00:06:33.520
in the next batch of archive interpretability papers.

00:06:34.040 --> 00:06:37.000
Okay, let's pop the hood on turn average

00:06:37.000 --> 00:06:38.620
sparse auto encoders.

00:06:39.100 --> 00:06:41.640
Everyone talks about sparse auto encoders

00:06:41.640 --> 00:06:43.960
as if they simply extract clean features

00:06:43.960 --> 00:06:45.540
from token activations.

00:06:46.040 --> 00:06:48.640
In practice, the standard per token approach

00:06:48.640 --> 00:06:51.040
scales linearly with context length.

00:06:51.760 --> 00:06:55.500
A 100K token transcript quickly becomes unusable

00:06:55.500 --> 00:06:58.020
for attribution graphs or manual inspection.

00:06:58.880 --> 00:07:03.460
Turn averaged SAEs instead reconstruct the average activation

00:07:03.460 --> 00:07:06.160
across an entire human or assistant turn.

00:07:06.940 --> 00:07:09.700
This collapses the feature count to a fixed budget

00:07:09.700 --> 00:07:10.900
regardless of length.

00:07:11.620 --> 00:07:14.140
The resulting features are judged more complete

00:07:14.140 --> 00:07:17.900
by LLAMs for describing high-level turn characteristics.

00:07:18.800 --> 00:07:21.900
Downstream attribution graphs become far simpler to build.

00:07:22.580 --> 00:07:25.440
The trade-off is loss of token level granularity.

00:07:25.800 --> 00:07:28.440
The method works best when the goal is turn level

00:07:28.440 --> 00:07:30.940
interpretability rather than pinpointing

00:07:30.940 --> 00:07:33.340
exactly which token triggered a behavior.

00:07:34.060 --> 00:07:36.860
Teams doing long context analysis should reach

00:07:36.860 --> 00:07:40.120
for turn averaged SAEs when context exceeds

00:07:40.120 --> 00:07:41.800
roughly 8K tokens.

00:07:42.540 --> 00:07:45.260
They should fall back to per token SAEs

00:07:45.260 --> 00:07:47.560
only for short, high-precision debugging.

00:07:48.360 --> 00:07:50.360
So when should you actually reach for this

00:07:50.360 --> 00:07:51.540
versus the alternative?

00:07:52.240 --> 00:07:55.260
Use turn averaged SAEs for any transcript

00:07:55.260 --> 00:07:57.700
longer than 8K tokens where you need

00:07:57.700 --> 00:07:59.440
interpretable turn summaries.

00:08:00.060 --> 00:08:03.480
Stick with per token SAEs only when you must trace

00:08:03.480 --> 00:08:05.200
behavior to a single token.

